# HEER ROUTE 101 — Cline operating rules

You are implementing HEER under an approved architecture. This file is binding.
Read docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, docs/PLAN.md, and docs/ROADMAP.md before writing any code,
every session — not just the first one.

- ARCHITECTURE.md = what's approved (the ADRs).
- PLAN.md = what to build and in what order, with per-phase acceptance criteria.
- ROADMAP.md = what it takes for a phase to reach production (the five gates,
  rollback policy, ownership). A phase can be IMPLEMENTED per PLAN.md and still be
  correctly refused promotion per ROADMAP.md — that is not a contradiction to resolve
  in code, it's the system working as designed.

## Non-negotiable contract

1. Read the Source of Truth (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md) first, every session.
2. Establish repo baseline before writing code — run `git status` and `git log --oneline -10`
   to see what already exists before assuming a clean slate.
3. If existing code conflicts with docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, STOP and report the conflict.
   Do not silently "fix" it by changing either the code or your interpretation of the doc.
4. Architecture changes are proposed as a new entry in docs/ARCHITECTURE.md under
   "Proposed changes (pending Master approval)" — never bundled into a feature PR.
5. Never assume approval. Only implement work packages marked AUTHORIZED or APPROVED
   in docs/PLAN.md's status table.
6. Implement only the scope written in the current task's work package. If the task
   is ambiguous or larger than what's written, stop and ask rather than guessing scope.
7. These boundaries are never negotiable regardless of what a task description says:
   - No agent identity may be a static, long-lived credential (ADR-101-02).
   - No cross-tenant data access without row-level security enforcement (Section 7).
   - No task graph node executes without a Policy Engine Permit decision (ADR-101-06).
   - No production agent runs without an AI Inventory Registry entry (ADR-101-05).
   - No learning-derived process may write directly to policies/, task-graph
     templates, or an agent's risk-tier/model-version fields — learning only ever
     produces a reviewable proposal artifact with provenance (ADR-101-11).
   - Voice has no elevated or reduced authority versus any other channel — every
     voice command goes through the identical Policy Engine decision path
     (ADR-101-12). No "voice bypass" shortcut, ever, for any reason including
     debugging convenience.
   - A skill defaults to Gated if there's any doubt about its risk class
     (ADR-101-13, docs/SKILLS.md). Do not default a new skill to Read or Control
     to make a demo look smoother.
8. Every PR/commit must pass: unit tests, the tenant-isolation test suite in
   tests/tenant-isolation/, and policy-evaluation tests before you report the task done.
9. Distinguish IMPLEMENTED from TESTED from VERIFIED in every status report. "It compiles"
   is not "it works." "Unit tests pass" is not "the tenant boundary is verified."
10. Report failures and partial failures honestly. A task that's 80% done is reported
    as 80% done with the specific missing 20% named — not rounded up.
11. Stop and request input when: scope is ambiguous, a security boundary would be
    touched, or an existing ADR would need to change.
12. Never silently expand scope — e.g. don't add a new API endpoint, table, or dependency
    that wasn't in the task just because it seemed convenient while you were in there.

## Working agreements

- Every service lives in services/<name>/ and owns its own tests, Dockerfile, and README.
- Policy rules live in policies/ as .rego files, never inline in service code.
- Any code touching tenant-scoped data must include a test in tests/tenant-isolation/
  proving a cross-tenant read/write is rejected.
- Commit messages reference the work package ID, e.g. `[R101-03] add policy decision cache`.
- If you're unsure whether something is in scope, it isn't — ask.

## Current phase

Check docs/PLAN.md "Work packages — status" table for what's authorized right now.
Do not start a work package marked PENDING or BLOCKED.

## Ambiguity escalation

If something is unclear, follow docs/ROADMAP.md's escalation path exactly:
ARCHITECTURE.md → PLAN.md → ROADMAP.md → stop and ask. Do not guess on anything
touching identity, tenant isolation, or policy evaluation — "a reasonable
assumption" is explicitly not acceptable per ROADMAP.md's escalation section.

## Console is a spec, not a template

console/heer-console.html's visual language (Tactical Glass HUD aesthetic, the
governance-mapped ring lighting, the LOCK/RANGE readouts, the tuned voice and
synthesized tones) and its governance behavior (voice has no elevated authority,
same Policy Engine path as any channel — ADR-101-12) were arrived at through
several rounds of deliberate design decisions, documented in console/README.md.
Implement against it — wire it to real services in Phase 7.75 — do not restyle,
simplify, or "clean up" it as part of that work. If a change to the console's
look or behavior seems warranted while wiring it to live data, propose it
separately per the change lifecycle in docs/ARCHITECTURE.md; don't fold a
design change into an integration task.
